WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.460
We've got one million Premier League seasons.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:10.920
So what's happened is me and me mate Adam, we've got together, and we've come up with a mathematical model and we've simulated

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:17.080
one million seasons in the English Premier League, which is the Football League that we have here in England, just to see what happens basically.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.060
Okay, so for those of you that don't know, eh this is how the, the, the Premier League works,

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.660
it's the top flight of English football or soccer as eh, Americans like to call it.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.820
And basically there are twenty teams, everybody plays each other home and away,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.200
and if you win the game you get 3 points, if you draw you get 1 points if you lose you don't get any points.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.800
It, you basically just tot up the points and see who's got the most points.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:49.270
If there's any sort of ties, so some teams have the same number of points, there's no playoffs or anything like that.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.509
It goes down to something called goal difference,

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.310
which is basically you take the number of goals that a team has scored and take off the number of goals

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:04.300
they've conceded. After that you go - it goes on goals scored. And

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.300
after that, it's just, they just agree a tie,

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:12.039
there's no ranking. And you can actually have literally two teams with identical records, in principle, and

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.159
literally, they won't be ranked. They'll just say, well, equal.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.280
Obviously the goal is to is to win, win the championship.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.880
But it's also really important to get into the top four

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.769
because the teams that get into the top four, they go into - there's something called the Champions league the next season.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.150
Which is really lucrative, loads of money.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.999
The big thing you want to avoid is

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.939
relegation, so that's finishing in the bottom three.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.530
If you finish in a bottom three

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.190
you actually drop down

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.440
to the division below - to the league below - the subsequent season and that's really bad because

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:52.010
obviously

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:57.889
it's nowhere near as glamorous, far less income - all together a whole lower standard of football and you don't want to be there basically.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.890
So that's the thing to avoid.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.309
So football fans everywhere, you know, we like to argue about you know,

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:12.110
how many points do you need to win the league, for example? I mean that's a, that's a big issue this season because you know

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.090
there's two teams that have got really great records,

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:19.490
they're Liverpool and Manchester City. And it looks like you're gonna need a lot of points to win the league this season.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.619
It's a thing you can ask: normally how many points would you need to win the league? How many points do you need to guarantee

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.100
entry into the Champions League the next season, to finish in that top four?

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:37.220
And of course the one that people really worry about is how many points do you actually need to avoid relegation?

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.989
What's - people talk about the magical forty point mark. So this is why we got interested,

00:02:41.989 --> 00:02:47.029
we were interested in asking these questions from a mathematical point of view. So, of course we could answer some of these questions

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:55.790
theoretically, but in not very interesting way. So for example, if you get 64 points you're guaranteed not to be relegated.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.580
That's a fact, right? You can show that quite easily.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:05.510
But of course anybody who gets 64 points, or 63 points actually, is not going to get relegated in any reasonable season.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:13.399
So we wanted to think more about probabilities and what, you know, what's likely to happen. But mathematically that's pretty much impossible to do

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:20.510
what we call analytically. So you really have to run computer simulations, use this idea of a Monte Carlo simulation.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:28.790
And you use sort of randomness to sort of try to create random seasons. And then over a lot of seasons the sort of

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:30.510
probabilistic

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.010
scenarios sort of play out. This idea of Monte Carlo simulations,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:38.539
they go back to the development of the atomic bomb actually. 
So we had a bit of a journey in

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.540
building up, building our model. We started off with some simpler models and then developed a quite sophisticated one

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.559
which is kind of based on the kind of thing that bookmakers use to predict outcomes of games.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:55.670
We used something called a Poisson distribution to sort of get the expected goals we said a given team would have in a given game,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:02.660
and built the model from that. And that used data from, from last season's Premier League actually.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:08.480
It's kind of a weird season in as much as Manchester City had an anomalously good season. They got 100 points.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.309
We're at the mercy of that, but we had to use something. And we literally played out the games in the simulation.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.609
So we did it, as I said, 380 games over a season - a

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.749
million seasons. It is quite quick. I mean, I'm not going to take the credit for this,

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.080
I'm sure if I wrote a bit code that tried to do this it would take forever,

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.580
but my friend Adam is much better at that sort of thing than me and was able to write a very efficient code

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.580
which did it in about 20 minutes, half an hour or so.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:34.849
And so we were really able to get out some fun statistics out of it.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:39.170
Basically how many points do you need to win the Premier League, on average, based on this simulation?

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:40.919
Well,

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:44.089
it turned out that the mean number of points that finished second place

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:49.520
was eighty five point two. So basically that's telling us that you if you get 86 points,

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.279
chances are you're gonna win the league. In reality,

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.140
you know, the team winning the league was getting 95 points or so, in this simulation. Again

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:03.080
that's because City did so well last season and we were using last season's data. To get the Champions League for example, on

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:09.680
average you would need 72 points according to this simulation, which is a reasonable figure. There are some

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:16.249
circumstances where you would, you would need a lot more or a lot less, but that was generally the ballpark. Now the crunch,

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:18.919
to avoid relegation.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:24.019
Well, we can look at the, at the number of points on average scored by the

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.970
the 18th team

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:29.239
so that's the the top team that did get relegated. And they got

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:35.359
32 and a half points was the mean. So that basically means that 33 points should be enough to avoid relegation

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:40.700
based on the sort of the simulations you would get from from from last season's data. That's quite low,

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.210
I think that's bit on the low side.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:47.150
But again, you know, if we'd averaged over more seasons with our data, probably we would have maybe got a higher figure there.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.550
So over a million seasons what sort of things happened?

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:52.840
So for example

00:05:52.840 --> 00:06:00.579
what's the most points that somebody won the league with? Needless to say Manchester City did on a, on actually on 62 occasions score

00:06:01.009 --> 00:06:03.639
114 points, which is where they won every single game.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:09.609
So 62 times out of a million season City got, you know, got all the points that were available. The highest number of points

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:14.770
somebody got relegated with? Okay, that would be a disaster, right? You could get relegated on a large number of points.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:21.220
Actually, it wasn't that high in these simulations. The highest number of points somebody got relegated with our million seasons

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:22.940
was

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:24.800
44.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.710
And actually that's that's kind of close to the sort of Premier League data. West Ham went down on 42.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:40.870
There was a season when teams played a few more games, where Crystal Palace went down on 49,

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:42.860
which was considered the ultimate disaster.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:49.389
(Brady: So in the million seasons there weren't any cases, really, where a team got relegated having a really good season?)

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:56.258
Nah, nah, not really. So other things that you notice: the most number of goals scored by a team in a season,

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.139
Manchester City on one particular season scored 161 goals.

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:08.769
Which blitzes the, the actual record which was set by Aston Villa in 1931, where they scored

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.679
128 goals. Again that was in 42 games.

00:07:12.379 --> 00:07:16.749
Somebody actually won the league in this as well only scoring 50 goals.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:24.699
Obviously Man City did annoying well. Out of the 1 million seasons, they won the league nearly 900 thousand times,

00:07:24.699 --> 00:07:27.878
they won it eight hundred ninety six thousand three hundred and thirteen times.

00:07:28.069 --> 00:07:30.549
Actually the team that won the next most, this is kind of curious,

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:35.259
you probably would think given that we were basing our last season data that the second-place team from last season,

00:07:35.259 --> 00:07:38.169
which was Manchester United, would win the next most. That's not what happened.

00:07:38.419 --> 00:07:42.038
The team that won the next most, which kind of pleases me, was Liverpool.

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:49.059
Okay, who finished fourth last season. They won the league in this 45 thousand eight hundred sixty-two times out of our simulation.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:55.089
So the reason the model favoured Liverpool was because when they won games they won by a lot. They scored a lot of goals,

00:07:55.089 --> 00:08:01.509
they won spectacularly when they won last season. So the model kind of likes that. 
- (Brady: Over the million seasons in your model)

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:08.020
(was there a freak season like the famous Leicester season where it, where an underdog came through and won it?)

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:13.270
So, not really, okay? So not in our, in the model simulation I'm talking about here.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:17.919
We did do a trial simulation where Leicester, and it was Leicester actually,

00:08:19.219 --> 00:08:24.309
won the league three times out of a million. In this simulation, this sort of main one, the final one,

00:08:24.319 --> 00:08:31.300
nobody outside of the top six won the league. Nobody. It's kind of depressing actually. 
- (Brady: Did any of the big super teams ever get relegated?)

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.060
They did yes. Yes, this is quite funny actually.

00:08:35.329 --> 00:08:42.639
Chelsea, poor old Chelsea, got relegated five? Five times? They finished second from bottom twice and they finished third from bottom three times.

00:08:42.709 --> 00:08:48.189
None of the other big six got relegated. There were a few sort of anomalous things. Southampton did surprisingly

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:48.490
well.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:54.909
They finished second once and they got into the Champions League - get this they got into Champions League 22 times out of a million!

00:08:55.310 --> 00:09:00.909
Okay, which is really, and they're completely anomalous. There's like, nobody else sort of that far down the table does anything like that

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:05.530
well. Again, it'll be because of the nature of it how their games played out,

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:08.589
they probably scored, they scored plenty goals - that kind of thing.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:14.229
So there's sort of a general overview of the season data, but there's some more fun extremes sitting in there

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:20.469
which we've got to talk about. What's the biggest win that anybody had in our simulation in our million seasons?

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:26.409
Well needless to say it was Manchester City. And they beat Bournemouth 20 goals to 1.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:36.159
So they scored 20 goals, Bournemouth got 1. That's blowing out of the water the sort of the kind of thing

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.620
we've actually seen in real life. The record

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:46.150
score in a top-flight game is 12 nil. Done by West Brom over 100 years ago, also Notting Forest

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:48.150
did it over 100 years ago.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:57.049
(Brady: But over the course of a million years, it's reasonable that it'll happen one day)
- It is, it is.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:02.930
I mean, it's not so crazy that City might occasionally get those those kind of score lines over a million seasons,

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:03.800
absolutely not.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:10.730
There were even more other remarkable score lines. Everton seemed to be heavily involved in some in some crazy score lines. The highest number of

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:16.969
goals by a losing team - ten. Chelsea played Everton at Goodison Park, Everton won 11-10. 

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:21.650
Okay, so that's a quick - and Everton seemed to be involved in a lot of these games.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:26.780
There was a Merseyside derby which was the highest scoring draw. That was Everton nine, Liverpool nine.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:32.840
I don't think my heart would be able to cope with a game

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:37.249
like that. I went to see Liverpool and Everton play and it was, it was a 4 all draw and that was bad enough.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.350
Nine all I don't think I could handle.

00:10:47.339 --> 00:10:53.149
The most goals in a game, okay. So the most goals in a game, that was Manchester City beat - again -

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.290
Everton, 17-5.

00:10:55.290 --> 00:11:00.710
22 goals in that game. There's actually, the record in English football that, in real English football not in our

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:06.679
simulation, was Tranmere Rovers Tranmere beat Oldham 13-4. So 17-5 is not

00:11:06.839 --> 00:11:10.309
actually that far away if you think about it. You know the model's not perfect.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:17.029
One thing it doesn't do very well, for example, is predict nil-nils. The reason for that is because a nil-nil draw is it's kind of - could be kind of a game of attrition.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:21.679
We, you know once a team gets sort of set in like that then that dictates what happens

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:29.029
next. And the model doesn't do that because it's really, really about randomness, okay, so it doesn't really capture nil-nils very well.

00:11:29.029 --> 00:11:34.819
So that's one of, one of the weaknesses of it. And actually it's not really clear how you would fix that, that's tricky.

